      "id": 1,
      "text": "Berclair sprang to life in 1889 when the railroad reached the spot, sparking a boom that drew workers and settlers alike.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berclair%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name blends the first names of local ranchers Bert and Clair Lucas, a nod to the community’s early pioneers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/berclair-tx",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1892 Berclair boasted a steam cotton gin, a saloon, a weekly newspaper called the Blossom, and eighteen other businesses for about 200 residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/berclair-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1920s saw Berclair grow to roughly 300 people, twenty businesses, and a designation as a banking town in both 1929 and 1931.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/berclair-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Even into the mid‑1980s, Berclair continued to supply surrounding ranches, keeping its rural roots alive.",
